**Claim 1.** A method for effecting fail-safe termination of drug infusion from an implanted drug delivery pump, the method comprising:

providing an implanted drug delivery pump having a fluid flow path configured to convey a drug from a reservoir to a patient, the fluid flow path comprising a flow-control member formed of a meltable hydrophobic material, the flow-control member defining a hole therethrough sized to permit passage of the drug through the fluid flow path at a normal, unmelted state of the meltable hydrophobic material;

positioning a ferromagnetic mesh in thermal communication with the flow-control member such that heat generated in the ferromagnetic mesh is transferable to the meltable hydrophobic material;

applying, from a location external to a body in which the pump is implanted, a time-varying magnetic field to the ferromagnetic mesh sufficient to inductively heat the ferromagnetic mesh to a temperature at or above a melting temperature of the meltable hydrophobic material;

conducting heat from the inductively heated ferromagnetic mesh to the meltable hydrophobic material to raise a temperature of the meltable hydrophobic material to or above its melting temperature; and

melting the meltable hydrophobic material in response to the conducted heat such that the melted hydrophobic material flows and closes the hole, thereby occluding the fluid flow path and terminating passage of the drug through the implanted pump.

# METHOD CLAIM FOR IMPLANTABLE DRUG DELIVERY SYSTEM WITH MAGNETIC EMERGENCY SHUT-OFF

## Claim 1

A method of implanting and operating a drug delivery system having an emergency shut-off feature, the method comprising:

**(a)** providing a drug delivery device comprising:
   (i) a reservoir configured to contain a therapeutic agent;
   (ii) a barrier member disposed between the reservoir and an outlet port, the barrier member comprising a hydrophobic meltable material defining at least one aperture therethrough, the at least one aperture configured to permit release of the therapeutic agent from the reservoir to the outlet port at a predetermined release rate;
   (iii) a magnetically-responsive closure element disposed proximate the at least one aperture, the magnetically-responsive closure element comprising a ferromagnetic or paramagnetic material embedded within or coupled to a meltable sealant material having a melting point compatible with the hydrophobic meltable material of the barrier member; and
   (iv) a heating element in thermal communication with the magnetically-responsive closure element, the heating element configured to generate localized heat sufficient to melt the meltable sealant material upon application of an external magnetic field;

**(b)** implanting the drug delivery device into a subcutaneous or intracorporeal site within a patient;

**(c)** releasing the therapeutic agent from the reservoir through the at least one aperture in the barrier member to the patient at the predetermined release rate over a treatment period;

**(d)** monitoring the patient for an occurrence of an adverse event associated with the therapeutic agent;

**(e)** upon detecting the adverse event, applying an external magnetic field to the drug delivery device from a location external to the patient, the external magnetic field being of sufficient strength and duration to actuate the heating element;

**(f)** activating the heating element in response to the external magnetic field, thereby generating heat proximate the at least one aperture;

**(g)** melting, via the heat generated in step (f), at least a portion of the meltable sealant material of the magnetically-responsive closure element and at least a portion of the hydrophobic meltable material surrounding the at least one aperture;

**(h)** flowing the melted sealant material and melted hydrophobic material into and across the at least one aperture under capillary action, thereby occluding the at least one aperture;

**(i)** re-solidifying the melted sealant material and the melted hydrophobic material within the at least one aperture upon cessation of the heat, thereby forming a solid seal that closes the at least one aperture; and

**(j)** terminating release of the therapeutic agent from the reservoir to the patient upon formation of the solid seal in step (i).

---

## Dependent Claims

**Claim 2.** The method of claim 1, wherein the external magnetic field of step (e) is generated by a handheld external magnet applicator positioned within a distance of about 1 cm to about 10 cm from the implanted drug delivery device.

**Claim 3.** The method of claim 1, wherein the magnetically-responsive closure element comprises iron oxide nanoparticles dispersed within a wax-based sealant material.

**Claim 4.** The method of claim 1, wherein the heating element comprises an inductive coil configured to generate heat via eddy current or hysteresis loss upon exposure to a time-varying external magnetic field.

**Claim 5.** The method of claim 4, wherein the external magnetic field applied in step (e) comprises an alternating magnetic field having a frequency in the range of about 50 kHz to about 500 kHz.

**Claim 6.** The method of claim 1, wherein the hydrophobic meltable material of the barrier member comprises a material selected from the group consisting of beeswax, paraffin wax, cetyl alcohol, glyceryl behenate, and combinations thereof.

**Claim 7.** The method of claim 1, wherein the adverse event of step (d) is detected by a biosensor integrated into the drug delivery device, the biosensor configured to measure a physiological parameter of the patient and to transmit a signal indicative of the adverse event to an external receiver.

**Claim 8.** The method of claim 1, further comprising, prior to step (e), transmitting a wireless alert to a caregiver or the patient indicating the occurrence of the adverse event.

**Claim 9.** The method of claim 1, wherein step (h) further comprises drawing the melted sealant material into the at least one aperture via a wicking structure disposed within or adjacent to the at least one aperture.

**Claim 10.** The method of claim 1, wherein the solid seal formed in step (i) is fluid-impermeable to the therapeutic agent.

**Claim 11.** The method of claim 1, wherein the drug delivery device further comprises a plurality of apertures in the barrier member, each aperture having a corresponding magnetically-responsive closure element, and wherein steps (f) through (i) are performed substantially simultaneously for the plurality of apertures upon application of the external magnetic field.

**Claim 12.** The method of claim 1, further comprising, after step (j), confirming closure of the at least one aperture by measuring, via a sensor integrated into the drug delivery device, a change in an electrical, optical, or mechanical property indicative of aperture occlusion.

**Claim 13.** The method of claim 1, wherein the therapeutic agent comprises an opioid analgesic, an anticoagulant, an insulin formulation, or a chemotherapeutic agent.

**Claim 14.** The method of claim 1, wherein the reservoir, the barrier member, and the magnetically-responsive closure element are housed within a biocompatible polymeric or metallic housing sized for subcutaneous implantation.
